THE ONE THAT MATTERED

`.env.example` listed POSTGRES_USER, POSTGRES_PASSWORD, API_PORT, SECRET_KEY,
DEBUG and CORS_ORIGINS. Not one of those is read anywhere in apps/web or
packages/*. They belong to `backend/` — a Python FastAPI project that is not
in pnpm-workspace.yaml, is not built by turbo, is not deployed by either
vercel.json, and shares no code with the site that serves ecowoods.ca.

So the single file whose entire job is telling a new contributor — or another
agent — what to configure was pointing at the wrong system, and the
forty-one variables the real application reads were written down nowhere.
That is worse than an empty file. An empty file makes somebody go and look.

It now lists every variable extracted from the source, grouped by what breaks
without it, because "optional" with no consequence attached reads as "ignore
this" and then something is quietly wrong in production for a month. The
entries that matter most are the ones with a silent failure mode: CRON_SECRET
unset means the hourly quote-recovery and review-request sweeps return 401
forever and nobody is told; no BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN and no Supabase key on
Vercel means generated PDFs are written to a disk that disappears.

FOUR DEAD PATH ALIASES

apps/web/tsconfig.json mapped @ecowoods/ui, @ecowoods/auth, @ecowoods/config
and @ecowoods/utils. Measured across apps and packages, excluding each
package's own directory: zero code imports each. `packages/utils` has no
source directory at all — only a package.json — so its alias pointed at a
path that does not exist, and `packages/config` holds one file nobody reads.

Removed. Zero importers means zero resolution change.

WHAT I FOUND AND DID NOT TOUCH, AND WHY

docs/REPO_STRUCTURE.md is new and carries the measurements. Three findings
are deliberately left alone:

`@ecowoods/shared` is aliased to ../../packages/shared/src. THAT DIRECTORY
DOES NOT EXIST — the package is flat. All 101 import sites work because
TypeScript falls through to the workspace link in node_modules, so the alias
is inert. Correcting it changes how a hundred imports resolve, and this
sandbox cannot run a production build to prove the new resolution is
identical. A config change that looks tidy and quietly alters module
resolution for a hundred files is the kind that gets discovered in
production.

@ecowoods/ui and @ecowoods/auth stay in apps/web/package.json. Removing them
edits the dependency graph and makes pnpm-lock.yaml stale; ship.sh runs a
plain `pnpm install` that currently reports "Lockfile is up to date,
resolution step is skipped". Spending a deploy cycle on two unused symlinks
is a bad trade. The doc carries the two-line command to do it properly with
pnpm regenerating the lockfile.

`backend/`, `apps/admin/` and the older React Native app in
apps/mobile/frontend/ are all dead by the same measure — nothing builds,
serves or imports them. None is deleted. Deleting somebody's Python backend
or their previous mobile app on the strength of "nothing imports it" is their
decision, not a tidiness patch's. They are written down so the choice is
deliberate rather than forgotten.

A GUARD WAS CONSIDERED AND NOT WRITTEN

`verify:workspace` — fail when a workspace package has zero importers, or a
tsconfig path points at a directory that is not there — would keep this true
without anybody re-reading it. It is not here, deliberately: the standing
instruction on this repository is not to add guards that stand between a
contributor and their work, and this one would fire on a new package the
moment it is created and before it is wired up. The structure is documented
instead, and the doc carries the argument for the guard if documentation
proves not to hold.

Verified: 65 of 66 guards. verify:css needs postcss from node_modules, absent
in this sandbox. repo hygiene clean. tsconfig.json still parses as JSON.
parse-scan 618 files, zero diagnostics. No source file changed, so no test
changed.

